Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ Sure, here's a simple C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text. This function uses a simple mapping for markdown to HTML conversion. For a more comprehensive conversion, you might want to use a library like `markdowncpp`.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> markdownToHtmlMap = {
        {"**", "<strong>"},
        {"*", "<em>"},
        {"#", "<h1>"}, // You can add more mappings for different heading levels
        {"[", "<a href='']"},
        {"]", "</a>"},
        {"<", "&lt;"},
        {">", "&gt;"}
    };

    std::stringstream html;
    std::string word;
    for (char c : markdown) {
        if (c == ' ') {
            if (!word.empty()) {
                html << word << " ";
                word.clear();
            }
        } else {
            word += c;
        }
    }
    if (!word.empty()) {
        html << word << " ";
    }

    std::string htmlString = html.str();
    for (const auto& pair : markdownToHtmlMap) {
        std::string key = pair.first;
        std::string value = pair.second;
        size_t pos = 0;
        while ((pos = htmlString.find(key, pos)) != std::string::npos) {
            htmlString.replace(pos, key.length(), value);
            pos += value.length();
        }
    }

    return htmlString;
}
```

This function works by replacing markdown syntax with corresponding HTML tags. Note that this is a very basic implementation and may not cover all markdown features. For a more comprehensive conversion, consider using a dedicated library.

