|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ People may have reservations about the many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics for several reasons. Firstly, it is inherently counterintuitive and challenges our everyday understanding of reality. The idea that every quantum event spawns an infinite number of parallel universes can be difficult to grasp and accept. Additionally, the many-worlds interpretation lacks direct empirical evidence, making it a subject of philosophical debate rather than a scientifically proven theory. Some critics also argue that it is unnecessarily complex and that simpler interpretations, such as the Copenhagen interpretation, are sufficient for practical purposes. Lastly, the concept of infinite universes raises questions about determinism and free will, which can be unsettling for some individuals.

